-- Conformance: a `data` name the compiler special-cases is reserved (Chapter 8, Section 8.4.1)
-- Tests: E158 — `data Tuple` is rejected at check (#1397).
-- The sibling of ch08_builtin_adt_redefinition_rejected, for the other
-- reserved name. `Tuple` is the variadic product carrier, recognised by
-- name at render time, so a declaration of it could not be told apart
-- from the built-in. Accepting it was silent: `show(MkShadow(7))` under
-- this declaration printed `(7)`, dropping the constructor name, and
-- `MkShadow(7) == MkShadow(7)` was refused against the BUILT-IN's non-Eq
-- fields. The prelude's data types (Option, Result, Ordering, UrlParts)
-- stay declarable: a program may shadow those, and §8.4.1 says so.
private data Tuple {
  MkShadow(Int)
}

public fn main(-> @Int)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  0
}
